uh the arden bomb shelter was created  and we don't know or i don't know the  exact year that it was created but it  was created to be  the headquarters  for all the bomb shelters in the vegas  valley and  for  the in in case of  an attack you know if the bomb shelter  system had to be activated  the  uh mayors the  county commission  uh the sheriff  the  police chiefs the  fire chiefs all of that would have  gathered at the arden bomb shelter  and they would have coordinated  um all the the efforts uh from that site  it was set up to handle  27 men and nine women  i'm not saying anything about the  equality there um  but that was set up to be it was it was  far enough away  in the 1950s  from where they thought the  bombs  would have hit  that it would have survived and it would  have been in touch with all of the other  bomb shelters in the valley and there  were quite a few of them every one of  the properties had a basement room that  was set up to be  a bomb shelter it was stocked in other  words  the  flamingo underpass the charleston  underpass both had bomb shelters set up  um there were  basically every abandoned mine that they  could they stocked with  non-perishable foods um so it was  that there was  areas set up all over the valley for  people to be able to get to and  hopefully  survive um for those of us that remember  those days um but there had to be  somewhere that was  the headquarters where everybody would  gather who was in charge and would be  able to then  coordinate  what was going on on up on the surface  well arden was where there was a cement  plant out there it was  a  mined uh uh gypsum out there but it was  miles away it was  quite a ways out it was a junction point  on the union pacific in fact that's  where they had decided was the closest  point that they could connect a railroad  to in order to bring supplies to the  hoover dam site  so that's where the the uh six companies  and the the railroad that went down to  the hoover dam site came off the  railroad out there  so it was quite a ways out  in in 19  well say the early 1950s  um we were probably somewhere around oh  i'm guessing here around 20 000 maybe  less than that  in the valley here so i figure we're  2.3 2.4 million at this point  20 000 at that point you're miles away  and and  they would have figured that  if  bombs were being dropped they would have  been dropped on  probably  nellis air force base it had changed to  nellis in 1950 so they would have aimed  at that  even if they had aimed at the airport  the airport was still very very little  so they probably wouldn't have been  worried about that you know they would  have aimed at  wherever there would have been some uh  possibility of  you know attacking back they wouldn't  have even aimed at the test site because  that was  literally only testing you you didn't  have nuclear weapons there they there  were nuclear weapons at mead view  out at lake mead which were  secretly there  and available then  to  nellis if they had to be  put on airplanes and  then  take off from nellis  but they would have aimed at nellis and  meadview not at  las vegas and  the little bit that henderson was at  that point henderson had only been  uh  incorporated in  1953 i think it was so you're not  talking about  a heavily populated area here this was a  very small area but the threat was  whether it was real or perceived it was  real  oh yeah but people felt the real threat  that they needed something like this  oh yes absolutely i mean i i was born in  1954 so i grew up  during this time when i was in grammar  school i had a bracelet that i wore that  had my name it had my address it had my  phone number it had my blood type on it  you know everything that was there in  case  you know there was a a  now i grew up in orange county  california um  but we did duck and cover drills just as  they were done here there were um  air raid sirens throughout the community  there's one of those that we saved and  restored out at the county museum you  can go out and actually see one of the  thunderers as the the type of air raid  siren it is but this was a very real  thing and and on both sides that that  sense that  could this happen absolutely  did anybody  really expect it to happen  i think  the interesting thing was i think among  us who were young  we lived with it and didn't really  expect it to happen  our parents  did they they had seen the bombs drop on  hiroshima  and nagasaki they they knew what that  was and and you know in the case of my  dad he was in training to go in on the  invasion on japan um he was in  underwater demolition in in the navy and  had done uh the invasions of iwo jima  and okinawa and so he knew what that  meant  um  but  when you grow up with it you just kind  of it's just part of the background of  growing up and so you just kind of  accept it  and the first time that i went into the  bomb shelter fallout shelter it's a real  place in  about 1990  seven 1996 somewhere back in there  um i hadn't been with the museums for  too long  at that point it was part of parks and  recreation  it was being leased from the  school district and being used by parks  and recreation they contacted the museum  and said  we've got a bomb shelter here with a  bunch of stuff in it would you like to  look at it  so i went with some of the other staff  and we looked at it and said yes we  would like to take selected artifacts so  that we can  you know  take these and and preserve them and and  have  enough pieces that we can talk about the  history of what this was and that's when  i got involved with the history of this  particular bomb shelter and started  doing some research on it and that  because my background is as an historian  i at the time was creating the aviation  museum at at the airport here  and um  we took a the the map that actually  showed all of the bomb shelter sites in  the valley here we took examples of the  uh radio equipment we took um  examples of the desks and chairs and  coveralls and all of that  what we left behind and what people see  in that video is actually not from  the time period of when it was a bomb  shelter  the the shelter was used in the  uh  80s and  throughout the 80s and even into the  late 80s  for  um exercises not for bomb shelter  exercises but for emergency exercises  the national guard used it and that sort  of thing so  that material is much later and so we  didn't need that it was not  it didn't reference  the period of time that the shelter was  built for so we didn't take that and and  it it just wasn't uh apropos to the  story that we wanted to document in the  history of that site so there's uh you  know they they had  a lot of uniforms left behind and that  sort of thing and they were  they weren't uniforms with any  particular insignia or anything they  were just dungarees and and you know  coveralls that sort of thing and and  there was some foodstuffs left behind  they weren't in any shape that you would  want to see  there were some some cans we took  example cans  but we didn't take all of them because  you only need one or two you don't need  15 of them you know it's it's something  where when you're  documenting a site you don't have to  empty the site to document it so the  other ones we just left there because  they weren't necessary for  documenting that site  the  difficulty with that site is that it's  an extremely toxic site  it's  the the site itself what they did when  they built that and it was typical of  the time  but they dug a big hole they used  railroad ties which were soaked with  creosote  at at that point in time i've worked  with creosote in my life when before i  was ever in the museum field um creosote  is a wonderful material if you don't  want bugs you know eating your railroad  ties  but they don't eat your railroad ties  because it's highly toxic and  carcinogenic  so  you know you can go down there and you  smell the creosote  then they were painted with lead-based  paint  so not only do you have a carcinogen  that you're breathing in you also have  this this the the standard really ugly  green lead-based paint on it  so  it  while  there is a a sort of sense that it would  be wonderful to be able to show people  that and walk through it  it would be very difficult to make it  safe for people to do it  to make it  ada accessible would be nearly  impossible because it was not built to  be  ada accessible literally you go down a  straight staircase you hit the bottom  there's a shower head over your head  you're supposed to strip down when you  get there and get showered off you walk  down a little alley the little corridor  very narrow too narrow for ada  not only the fact that you're going down  steps  and then you  put on these coveralls that are right  there in just a cubby and then you go  around the corner and you're in this  enclosed space  where if you were down there with 36  other people  within a week or two  you'd probably be heading back up on the  surface you know if if you have any  claustrophobia i i do you know i'd be  heading up you know it's one of those  that it would be very hard  to spend a long period of time down  there  plus the fact that if you did  you're setting yourself up for cancer in  the future and a lot of other problems  um  and trying to make this available to the  public you know because i've worked with  a lot of historic structures  yeah  i'm not sure how you'd be able to do  that you know there there are  interesting historic sites i would  almost rather take one of the  abandoned mines  that are still out there with with  stocks of food in them  and say  this is the kind of thing that we did  this is how we were trying  to deal with  you know the idea of bombing
 

